WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.730
Sekigawata Middle School 
Sekigawata High School

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:05.730
4 Schools Quiz Meet 
Round 2 begins at 13:00

00:00:02.020 --> 00:00:05.730
Round 2 will begin at 1 p.m., after lunch.

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:09.440
To get to the convenience store, go out of 
the gate, turn right, and walk for 200 meters.

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.950
That's right... I need to go buy lunch...

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:17.620
Want to share my lunch with me?

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:18.370
Huh?!

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:23.250
I ended up with a little too much.

00:00:23.430 --> 00:00:27.650
Koshiyama!

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:29.840
Do you want some, too, Inoue-kun?

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.800
Thank you!

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:38.140
I'm so happy I get to eat your 
homemade lunch, Fukami-san!

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:42.730
Actually, my mom made it. I only packed it.

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.810
I'm so happy!

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.210
Thank you for sharing.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.770
I'm going to buy a drink 
and get some fresh air.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.680
I hope he's not planning 
on cutting and running...

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.940
H-He wouldn't do that!

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.350
<b>Fastest
Finger
First</b>

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.960
<b>Question 3: "If You Want the Right to Answer<i> /</i>"</b>

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:35.050
What is quiz bowl, anyway?

00:02:35.050 --> 00:02:40.380
Quiz bowl is a game known by 
everyone around the world.

00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:42.380
Sasajima-senpai...

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.630
Reading a book of quiz 
questions? That's good.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.600
Question collections like that 
include a lot of classics.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:54.060
If I'd known it would be like this,
 I would've memorized them in advance.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.570
If you don't know the question, 
you can't buzz in, right?

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:00.800
Disappointed to learn what it's all about?

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:04.500
Not so much disappointed... Just, well...

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:07.830
It's not what I expected quiz bowl to be.

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:11.690
It's true that if you only look at the 
classics, it seems less like quiz bowl

00:03:11.210 --> 00:03:15.420
The fall paddy shacks have rough
thatching as my sleeves are wet with dew

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:15.090
and more like the Hundred Poets game, where 
you grab the second verse based on the first.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:15.420
<b>as my sleeves 
are wet with dew</b>

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.540
But there is one marked difference.

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.470
The classics aren't limited to 100.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:25.530
They're constantly being updated, and there 
are different ways of asking them, too.

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:26.600
But...

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:30.710
Thousands of questions could 
potentially be regarded classics.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:32.940
No one person can memorize them all.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.220
But everyone else was buzzing early.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.400
I don't think I can...

00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:38.110
I don't know about that.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:41.060
Competitions are a matter of chance.

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:43.630
They're fun because you never 
know what can happen.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:47.830
There are those who can still buzz in 
without knowing the classics.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:53.000
I expect a good showing in the next round.

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:56.170
<i>Easy for him to say...</i>

00:03:57.510 --> 00:04:02.290
The second round will see the winners of 
Round 1 take part in a "fastest finger board."

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:06.060
Only three competitors will advance 
through to the final round!

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.070
Here are the rules. A "fastest finger board"

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.560
combines elements from the fastest finger 
quiz bowl and the board-style quiz bowl.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:17.840
As in the "fastest finger" format, when a 
contestant knows an answer, they buzz.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.380
At that point, the question
 will stop being read.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:25.300
Then, instead of getting the contestant 
who buzzed to say the answer,

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:28.050
all six contestants write their
answers on a whiteboard.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.370
<b>Right Answer</b>

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:30.370
<b>ht Answer</b>

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:38.380
<b>Wrong Answer</b>

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:30.670
If the person who buzzed guesses 
correctly, they get three points.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:30.490
<b>ight Answer</b>

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:30.490
<b>Right Ans</b>

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.620
<b>Right Answer</b>

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:33.620
<b>Right A</b>

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:33.920
Even those who didn't buzz can earn 
one point with a correct answer.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:33.750
<b>t Answer</b>

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:33.870
<b>Right Answer</b>

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:33.870
<b>nswer</b>

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.870
<b>wer</b>

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:39.170
<b>Wrong Answer</b>

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:37.950
But if the one who buzzed guesses 
wrong, they lose three points.

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:40.250
<b>Right Answer</b>

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.960
Contestants who didn't buzz are 
not penalized for a wrong answer.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:43.670
You'll have ten seconds to consider your answers.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.410
<i>So everyone has the right to 
answer, even if they don't buzz in?</i>

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:49.320
<i>Maybe I can do this, after all.</i>

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.780
There will be 15 questions.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.640
The three with the most points 
at the end will advance to the final.

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:57.860
Now, let's proceed with the buzzer test.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:01.110
<i>Good!</i>

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:08.030
Round 2 will now begin!

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:09.500
Question.

00:05:09.810 --> 00:05:14.480
Used in tying knots such as the clove hitch,
 the figure eight, and the Prusik knot,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:15.490
what mountaineering rope—

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:20.360
<i>Way to go, Fukami-san!</i>

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.570
I know this one now.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:23.680
Show your boards!

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:24.630
Seil

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:27.090
"Seil." All contestants are correct!

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:30.960
Damn Koshiyama... 
He's supposed to be a newbie!

00:05:31.270 --> 00:05:31.810
Question.

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:35.860
Located in South Dakota, America, the 
four former presidents: Washington,

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:38.770
Jefferson, Roosevelt, and Lincoln are—

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:42.710
<i>The four former presidents listed are...</i>

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.990
The correct answer is Mount Rushmore.

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:44.280
<b>Mount Rushmore</b>

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:48.450
Sorry, you two from Sekigawata.

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:49.890
Question.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:54.170
The first person to reach the South Pole 
was Norwegian explorer, Amundsen. But which—

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:58.000
Why did he buzz before 
the determining point?

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.460
Looks like Mikuriya's getting going.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.320
<i>Questions continuing from
a connector like "while" or "and"</i>

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.820
<i>have some kind of connection to the first part,</i>

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.930
<i>but the question's asking
something different, right?</i>

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:10.510
<b>Uemura Naomi</b>

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:11.850
<b>Scott</b>

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:11.850
<b>Peary</b>

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.180
<b>Peary</b>

00:06:11.850 --> 00:06:13.640
<b>Scott</b>

00:06:12.160 --> 00:06:14.560
The correct answer is Peary!

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:14.560
<b>Peary</b>

00:06:15.170 --> 00:06:20.050
The first person to reach the South Pole 
was Norwegian explorer, Amundsen.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:24.870
But which American explorer was 
the first to reach the North Pole?

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:26.150
That's the question in full.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:28.400
Oh, it was that one...

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.870
<i>I thought it was asking who the second 
person to reach the South Pole was...</i>

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.770
<i>How did he know it would 
be about the North Pole?</i>

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:36.240
Question.

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:40.600
The first shogun of the Kamakura shogunate 
was Minamoto no Yoritomo. But who—

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.140
Huh?

00:06:42.140 --> 00:06:43.420
Seriously, dude. Too soon.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.840
<i>Nice going, question reader.</i>

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:47.210
Show your boards!

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:49.170
<b>Hojo Masako</b>

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:49.170
<b>Prince Morikumi</b>

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.220
This means...

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:51.050
<b>Hojo Tokimasa</b>

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:52.010
Mikuriya-kun's correct!

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.930
<b>Tokugawa Ieyasu</b>

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:53.750
The question was,

00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:57.490
"The first shogun of the 
Kamakura shogunate was Minamoto no Yoritomo.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:07:00.140
But who was the first regent?"

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:03.500
I can't even read what Koshiyama wrote.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:05.540
He wrote "Prince Morikumi."

00:07:05.540 --> 00:07:09.110
He heard "first shogun," so he answered 
with the ninth and last shogun.

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.960
How did Mikuriya know that answer?

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:13.360
It's all about accentuation.

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:17.240
"Parallel questions" are a type of question in 
which a word given as a hint in the first half

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.220
is changed in the second half

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:22.250
to ask a different, but related, question.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.090
But sometimes, the person
 reading the question

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.010
will actually emphasize 
the keyword as a hint.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:30.700
Since he placed emphasis on "shogun,"

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:34.660
Mikuriya predicted that the second 
half would ask for "the first something."

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.470
<b>The first shogun of the Kamakura shogunate
 was Minamoto no Yoritomo.</b>

00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:39.570
In parallel questions about officials 
in the Kamakura Shogunate,

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:38.520
<b>The first shogun of the Kamakura shogunate
 was Minamoto no Yoritomo.</b>

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.730
<b>The first shogun of the Kamakura shogunate 
was Minamoto no Yoritomo. 
But who was the first regent?</b>

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.280
He scares me, and I'm on his team.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.570
And the way he shows no mercy to newbies 
shows his lack of maturity as a quizzer.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:54.740
<i>Not even letting the others 
hear the whole question</i>

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:56.860
<i>is just another tactic.</i>

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:58.670
Question.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:01.800
Beethoven's 3rd Symphony is 
titled "Eroica." Wh—

00:08:02.360 --> 00:08:04.560
<i>I couldn't hear the accentuation.</i>

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.920
<i>Did I miss it?</i>

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.690
<i>Beethoven's 3rd Symphony...</i>

00:08:08.690 --> 00:08:10.380
<i>A different question connected to that...</i>

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:14.150
<i>If you think all questions connected by "and" are parallel, you're dead wrong.</i>

00:08:14.150 --> 00:08:16.350
The correct answer is Napoleon!

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:16.350
Napoleon

00:08:16.350 --> 00:08:17.390
Huh?!

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:21.180
"Who does the title refer to?" 
was the question in full.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:23.560
Only Mikuriya-kun guessed correctly!

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.210
<i>I get it... That time, the info 
just prefaced the question.</i>

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:29.780
<i>That's why he didn't emphasize 
any particular word.</i>

00:08:29.780 --> 00:08:32.570
<i>That wasn't the question 
I was expecting at all.</i>

00:08:32.570 --> 00:08:34.990
<i>I can't predict what the 
questions are going to be...</i>

00:08:37.030 --> 00:08:39.720
<i>It's no use! I can't make 
sense of any of these!</i>

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:42.760
<i>Maybe I should give everyone 
else a chance to get one right.</i>

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.130
<i>This kid next to me is getting even smaller.</i>

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:49.630
<i>His answers have all been 
really close, though...</i>

00:08:52.300 --> 00:08:53.050
Question.

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.770
<i>Absent in the Spring</i> is one of 
many romance novels by—

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:01.910
<i>Absent in the Spring</i> was 
written by Agatha Christie.

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.180
<i>Wait...</i>

00:09:03.180 --> 00:09:07.520
<b>Agatha</b>

00:09:03.910 --> 00:09:07.240
<i>The question's actually given 
the title of the novel...</i>

00:09:07.740 --> 00:09:09.980
<i>Of course! This </i>must<i> be the answer!</i>

00:09:09.980 --> 00:09:11.110
<b>Agatha Christie</b>

00:09:11.110 --> 00:09:12.070
<b>Agatha Christie</b>

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:12.780
<b>Agatha Christie</b>

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:14.360
<b>Agatha Christie</b>

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:16.320
<b>Mary Westmacott</b>

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.410
The correct answer is Agatha Christie.

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:21.290
No way! I was so sure!

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.870
<i>He really is...</i>

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:30.400
The question was, "<i>Absent in the 
Spring</i> is one of many romance novels

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:33.130
by the so-called mother of Hercule Poirot,

00:09:32.380 --> 00:09:35.090
<b>Mary Westmacott</b>

00:09:33.130 --> 00:09:35.090
a character created by 
which English mystery writer?"

00:09:35.090 --> 00:09:38.230
<i>For him to come up with Mary 
Westmacott so naturally...</i>

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:44.250
"Mary Westmacott" was the 
pen name Christie used

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.620
when she wrote romance novels, so that 
readers wouldn't confuse them for mysteries.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:50.970
Isn't that a correct answer too, then?

00:09:51.330 --> 00:09:55.380
The question asked which mystery 
author created Poirot.

00:09:55.380 --> 00:09:57.280
It is still judged as being incorrect.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.640
<i>What is this feeling?</i>

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:01.550
<i>I've never felt like this before.</i>

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:05.200
What a tantalizing expression.

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:08.170
<i>He's thinking along the right lines...</i>

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:11.200
<i>If I can just predict the rest 
of the question, I can do this!</i>

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:13.000
<i>I can get the right answers!</i>

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:16.210
<i>Guessing wrong when I know 
the right answer is infuriating!</i>

00:10:17.410 --> 00:10:19.170
Four questions remain!

00:10:19.170 --> 00:10:21.350
Be careful, but be bold!

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:22.230
Question.

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:25.930
<i>Summer Wars</i> and <i>The Girl 
Who Leapt Through Time</i> are—

00:10:26.430 --> 00:10:28.380
<i>Summer War</i>?

00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:32.060
The Girl Who Leapt Through Time<i> was 
written by Tsutsui Yasutaka, wasn't it?</i>

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:33.680
Show your boards!

00:10:33.270 --> 00:10:37.030
<b>Sadamoto Yoshiyuki</b>

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:35.650
The correct answer is Hosoda Mamoru.

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:37.030
What?!

00:10:37.030 --> 00:10:41.410
"<i>Summer Wars</i> and <i>The Girl Who 
Leapt Through Time</i> are two movies

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.490
<b>Hosoda Mamoru</b>

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:42.990
<b>Hosoda Mamoru</b>

00:10:41.560 --> 00:10:45.910
directed by which renowned 
Japanese anime director?"

00:10:41.990 --> 00:10:43.990
<b>Tsutsui Yasutaka</b>

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:45.910
<b>Sadamoto Yoshiyuki</b>

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:48.410
Ohbayashi... Good taste.

00:10:48.410 --> 00:10:50.820
<i>He clearly knows a lot. It's a real shame.</i>

00:10:51.130 --> 00:10:54.330
<i>If he had the ability to predict 
the rest of the question...</i>

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:56.300
Question.

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:02.360
The 19th Century musician, Liszt, was known
 as the "<i>magician</i> of the piano." But who—

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:04.760
<i>That was blatant.</i>

00:11:05.330 --> 00:11:07.440
<i>That was a funny way to read the question.</i>

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.670
<i>If it's asking a separate 
question in the second part,</i>

00:11:11.670 --> 00:11:14.900
<i>then riffing off of "magician," 
maybe it'll be "poet"?</i>

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:17.160
<i>Or is it the "magician of the violin"?</i>

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:21.180
If only I could have heard 
a "p" or "v" before he buzzed in...

00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:23.490
<i>The way he read it...</i>

00:11:23.920 --> 00:11:25.750
The first person to 
reach the <i>South</i> Pole...

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.030
<i>He did it in that question, too.</i>

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:28.680
The first <i>shogun</i> of...

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:29.510
<i>And that one!</i>

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:32.710
<i>They were read that way, too!</i>

00:11:32.960 --> 00:11:34.670
That was an obvious one.

00:11:34.670 --> 00:11:35.220
Huh?

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:36.420
Show your boards!

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:37.860
The correct answer is Chopin!

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.130
<b>Chopin</b>

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.310
<i>Y-Yes!</i>

00:11:39.310 --> 00:11:44.530
"The 19th Century musician, Liszt, was known
 as the '<i>magician</i> of the piano.'

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:47.890
<b>Mozart</b>

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:47.890
<b>Chopin</b>

00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:47.030
But who was known as 
the 'poet of the piano'?"

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.700
By intentionally adding specific 
intonation and emphasis,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:55.290
the reader flagged up what question 
would be asked in the second part.

00:11:55.290 --> 00:11:57.390
Oh, the "magician" thing?

00:11:57.390 --> 00:11:58.360
Yes.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.860
<i>I was right!</i>

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:02.500
<i>There was a hint in the 
way he read the question!</i>

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:05.320
<i>Did Mikuriya-kun notice how it was read?</i>

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:07.790
<i>He's finally caught on...</i>

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:09.560
An accentuation, huh?

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.290
It wasn't easy to make an accurate guess 
from the way he read that, though.

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.820
Not surprising from Sekigawata, 
a school famous for its quiz bowl team.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:18.000
Question.

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:19.960
<i>Pay attention to the way he reads it, too!</i>

00:12:20.940 --> 00:12:25.030
A lone woman in a group of men is 
known as a "single bright flower."

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:28.270
In the Wang Anshi poem, said to be—

00:12:29.720 --> 00:12:32.140
<i>Yes! I was able to buzz in!</i>

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.350
<i>Buzzing in really does feel good!</i>

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.360
<b>A Single Bright Flower 
in the Green</b>

00:12:37.160 --> 00:12:41.070
<i>I predicted that the question would ask for 
the relevant verse in the Wang Anshi poem.</i>

00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:44.330
<i>That would be "a single bright 
flower in the greenery."</i>

00:12:42.700 --> 00:12:48.620
<b>A Single Bright Flower in the Greenery</b>

00:12:44.330 --> 00:12:46.280
<i>The poem is about 
a lone pomegranate blossom,</i>

00:12:46.280 --> 00:12:48.620
<i>blooming in an expanse of green leaves.</i>

00:12:49.100 --> 00:12:50.910
<i>That flower is the "single bright flower."</i>

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:55.670
<b>A Single Bright Flower
 in the Greenery</b>

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:52.970
<i>This has to be right.</i>

00:12:53.370 --> 00:12:55.670
<i>But... why does something 
feel off about this?</i>

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:57.800
<i>Think hard!</i>

00:12:56.880 --> 00:12:59.040
Agatha Christie                              Mary Westmacott

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.730
<i>All the answers I've tried 
to guess have been wrong.</i>

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.590
Prince Morikumi                           Hojo Tokimasa

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:01.500
<i>Why?</i>

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.590
<b>A Single Bright Flower 
in the Greenery</b>

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:05.590
<i>They're easy questions, 
but I'm making them harder...</i>

00:13:05.990 --> 00:13:07.130
<i>Easy?</i>

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:08.630
The earlier in the meet you are,

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.490
the more likely you are to get classic 
questions with simple construction.

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:16.330
<i>I just have to go for it!</i>

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.980
<b>Hey... Have you made friends in your class? 
Do you eat lunch with them? 
You're not still asking quiz questions
 anywhere and anytime, are you? 
I'm worried about you!</b>

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:20.980
Nervous

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.990
...to New York!

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.990
Welcome...

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.990
Ba-dump 
Ba-dump

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.990
Stretch...

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:24.950
Show your boards!

00:13:26.200 --> 00:13:28.160
<b>Pomegranate</b>

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:28.160
The correct answer is pomegranate!

00:13:26.950 --> 00:13:28.160
<b>Green</b>

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:28.160
<b>Pomegranate</b>

00:13:28.160 --> 00:13:28.950
<b>Pomegranate</b>

00:13:30.660 --> 00:13:33.750
I was right! My prediction was right!

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:35.250
The complete question was,

00:13:35.250 --> 00:13:39.000
"A lone woman in a group of men is 
known as a 'single bright flower.'

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:43.100
In the Wang Anshi poem, said to 
be the origin of this phrase,

00:13:43.100 --> 00:13:45.760
what kind of flower was the 
'single bright flower'?"

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:48.260
<i>He wasn't getting anything right before...</i>

00:13:50.740 --> 00:13:52.360
And we've arrived at the last question!

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:54.480
It's a close race to make 
it into the top three!

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:56.690
<i>The end already?</i>

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.740
<i>I'm in second. I'm guaranteed to advance.</i>

00:13:59.740 --> 00:14:01.190
<i>I just need to hold my position.</i>

00:14:01.570 --> 00:14:04.900
<i>I need to avoid buzzing and 
just go for the single point.</i>

00:14:04.900 --> 00:14:08.160
<i>But if Maririn gets three 
points, I'll drop below her!</i>

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:11.050
<i>I need to buzz in and get three 
points to get into third place.</i>

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:12.410
<i>That's my only chance to win!</i>

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.500
<i>What kind of question will be next?</i>

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:15.620
<i>Will I get to buzz again?</i>

00:14:15.960 --> 00:14:18.170
<i>What's the point in getting 
all serious about this?</i>

00:14:18.930 --> 00:14:19.710
Question.

00:14:20.230 --> 00:14:25.150
Michelangelo's "The Last Judgment" can be 
found in the Sistine Chapel. But Da—

00:14:25.710 --> 00:14:26.910
<i>Mikuriya-kun?!</i>

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.030
<i>I thought he wouldn't bother 
buzzing on this one!</i>

00:14:31.060 --> 00:14:35.850
<i>After he said "Da," I think I heard 
him forming the sound of a "v"...</i>

00:14:36.590 --> 00:14:37.520
<i>The statue of David!</i>

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.230
<i>It fits with Michelangelo, 
so it has to be right!</i>

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:41.310
<i>This is the answer!</i>

00:14:41.310 --> 00:14:44.110
<i>The statue of David is in the 
Galleria dell'Accademia!</i>

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:44.530
<b>Galleria dell'Accademia</b>

00:14:44.110 --> 00:14:47.070
The correct answer is 
Santa Maria delle Grazie!

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.070
<b>Santa Maria delle Grazie</b>

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:48.370
What?!

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:50.590
The rest of that question was,

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:56.120
"But da Vinci's 'The Last Supper' can 
be found in which church in Milan?"

00:14:56.120 --> 00:14:58.710
<i>It wasn't David, but da Vinci?!</i>

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:03.260
<i>And they're even called "The Last 
Judgment" and "The Last Supper"!</i>

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:02.040
<b>The Last Judgment</b>

00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.670
<b>The Last Supper</b>

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:05.730
Mikuriya-kun of Miyaura High School,

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Fastest Finger Board Quiz (6 -> 3)</b>

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Sekigawata MS

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Buzou High

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Miyaura High

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Buzou High

00:15:03.670 --> 00:15:07.670
<b>Asagaoka Girls'

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.670
Sasaki-kun of Sekigawata Middle School,

00:15:07.940 --> 00:15:10.030
and Kouzuki-san of Asagaoka 
Girls' High School

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.890
advance to the final round!

00:15:12.560 --> 00:15:14.850
Hey, you alive?

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:19.250
Learn to take a hint, would you?

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.560
What's the problem? I wanted to buzz in.

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.470
Congratulations, Yukicchi! 
You have to win now!

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:30.030
Yeah! Those two seem really 
good, but I'll give it my best shot!

00:15:30.710 --> 00:15:34.840
<i>I think I understand why people care 
so deeply about quiz bowl now.</i>

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:38.060
<i>I've only taken part in a few quizzes,</i>

00:15:38.530 --> 00:15:39.950
<i>but every time I do one,</i>

00:15:39.950 --> 00:15:42.070
<i>I start to see a world I never knew before.</i>

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.420
Koshiyama, you'll need to 
start with the basics.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:49.790
There was no way David would be 
the answer to that last one.

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:52.020
I know <i>I'd</i> never ask 
a question like that, though.

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:52.800
Oh...

00:15:53.510 --> 00:15:58.260
There's no connection between paintings 
and sculptures, nor chapels and museums.

00:15:58.650 --> 00:16:00.680
There's no artistry in a parallel like that!

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:02.900
<i>Who cares about artistry?!</i>

00:16:02.900 --> 00:16:06.110
<b>Sekigawata Middle School 
Sekigawata High School</b>

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.110
Welcome, young players.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:10.490
The moment you took that first 
step into the world of quiz bowl,

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:14.340
you became fellow members 
of our quiz cluster!

00:16:14.340 --> 00:16:16.480
After knocking it out of the park 
against a field of rookies,

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.580
these three have advanced 
through to the final round!

00:16:19.960 --> 00:16:24.420
The Four Schools Quiz Meet to decide the King 
of the Newcomers has reached its final stage!

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:26.380
The rule of this round is "five right, two wrong."

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:27.520
"Five right, two wrong"?

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:30.340
The first to five correct answers wins.

00:16:30.340 --> 00:16:34.010
But answer two questions wrong 
and you'll be eliminated.

00:16:34.610 --> 00:16:36.400
Oh, so that's what it means!

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:38.220
You wanted to keep playing?

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:41.310
Huh? Uh, no, I'm worn out for today...

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:46.930
Besides, quiz bowl is a lot 
harder than I thought.

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.140
That's true.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:53.470
It's not so easy that a newcomer 
school like ours could come here and win.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.490
<b>Final Round  Five Right, Two Wrong 
Fastest Finger Quiz (3 -> 1)</b>

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.490
<b>Miyaura High School

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.490
<b>Sekigawata Middle School

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:55.490
<b>Asagaoka Girls'
High School

00:16:54.710 --> 00:16:55.490
Still...

00:16:55.850 --> 00:16:57.210
It was fun.

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:57.950
Yeah.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:00.830
Question.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.250
Known as "The Abbey,"

00:17:03.760 --> 00:17:07.840
the church in London that houses 
the graves of Newton and Darwin,

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:10.380
and where many British monarchs 
have had their coronations—

00:17:11.320 --> 00:17:12.640
Westminster Abbey.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.050
Correct!

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:16.800
<i>After hearing all that, 
even I knew the answer.</i>

00:17:17.890 --> 00:17:20.870
<i>That question was longer 
than the ones in round 2.</i>

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.310
<i>I guess Mikuriya-kun waited to hear 
most of the question before buzzing</i>

00:17:24.530 --> 00:17:28.520
<i>because no clear hints were 
given until the very end.</i>

00:17:29.070 --> 00:17:29.810
Question.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:34.650
The rule that warm-blooded animals of the 
same taxonomical clade living in cold areas—

00:17:35.320 --> 00:17:37.950
Uh... Allen's rule!

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:38.950
Sorry!

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:41.410
<b>Q: The rule that warm-blooded animals 
of the same taxonomical clade living in cold areas are larger than those living 
in warm areas is known by what name?</b>

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:41.410
<b>A. Bergmann's rule</b>

00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:41.410
The answer is Bergmann's rule.

00:17:41.810 --> 00:17:44.930
<i>The latter rounds really are tough.</i>

00:17:45.540 --> 00:17:46.270
Question.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:51.350
Made famous as the theme of Wagner's 
<i>The Ring of the Nibelung</i>,

00:17:51.350 --> 00:17:53.280
what word from Norse mythology 
signifies the—

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.030
Sieg—

00:17:56.770 --> 00:18:00.780
<i></i>The Ring of the Nibelung<i> is an 
epic performed over four nights.</i>

00:18:01.070 --> 00:18:02.960
<i>The prelude is </i>The Rhinegold<i>.</i>

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:04.810
<i>The first night is </i>The Valkyrie<i>.</i>

00:18:04.810 --> 00:18:06.600
<i>The second night is </i>Siegfried<i>...</i>

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:11.360
<i>Another word that originated from
Norse mythology is "Valkyrie"...</i>

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:15.440
Oh? Is Mikuriya about to get his 
first wrong answer of the day?

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.230
<i>The third night, the final 
act, is </i>Twilight of the Gods<i>.</i>

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.940
<i>In the end, the final battle 
between the gods and the giants</i>

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:23.650
<i>turns everything into a sea of fire,</i>

00:18:23.650 --> 00:18:25.450
<i>and the world meets its end.</i>

00:18:26.250 --> 00:18:27.250
<b>The World's End</b>

00:18:27.170 --> 00:18:28.950
The world's end...

00:18:27.710 --> 00:18:28.710
<b>Twilight of the Gods</b>

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:30.870
Twilight of the Gods...

00:18:29.630 --> 00:18:31.880
<b>The day of the end...</b>

00:18:30.870 --> 00:18:31.880
The day of the end...

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:33.460
Ragnarok!

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:46.160
Uh... That question won't be counted.

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:48.950
I'm so sorry!

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:51.610
We'll be more careful.

00:18:54.610 --> 00:18:56.900
Koshiyama, we're leaving.

00:18:57.260 --> 00:18:59.660
Oh, there's no need to go that far...

00:18:59.870 --> 00:19:02.360
No, that's the rule.

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:11.290
<b>Q: Made famous as the theme of Wagner's 
<i>The Ring of the Nibelung</i>, what word from Norse 
mythology signifies the end of the world 
and means "Twilight of the Gods"?</b>

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:09.920
Ragnarok was the correct answer, wasn't it?

00:19:09.830 --> 00:19:11.290
<b>A: Ragnarok</b>

00:19:09.920 --> 00:19:11.290
Yeah, it was.

00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:14.430
<i>He arrived at the right answer before I did!</i>

00:19:15.740 --> 00:19:17.840
Um... I'm sorry.

00:19:18.640 --> 00:19:21.890
It's my fault for not teaching 
you the proper etiquette.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:26.100
No, it's obvious to anyone that 
I shouldn't have shouted the answer out.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.100
The only ones allowed to answer are 
those who have earned the right to.

00:19:30.470 --> 00:19:33.340
Reacting in a way that 
suggests you know the answer

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.570
and pretending to press a buzzer 
button are inappropriate.

00:19:36.570 --> 00:19:37.670
I'm sorry.

00:19:38.970 --> 00:19:40.650
Do you want the right to answer?

00:19:40.650 --> 00:19:41.070
Huh?

00:19:41.330 --> 00:19:43.350
If you do, improve your technique,

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:44.780
store up more knowledge...

00:19:46.080 --> 00:19:47.750
and learn the ways of quiz bowl.

00:19:50.970 --> 00:19:53.840
Quiz bowl is more than just 
showing off your knowledge.

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:56.920
It includes many rules and strategies.

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:01.510
Enjoying the game and competing to 
win is what quiz bowl means to us.

00:20:02.030 --> 00:20:03.420
That's competitive quiz bowl.

00:20:07.630 --> 00:20:11.270
The King of the Newcomers is 
Mikuriya-kun of Miyaura High School!

00:20:12.750 --> 00:20:13.760
Sounds like it's all over.

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:16.530
I wish I could've watched it to the end...

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.900
<b>Yukicchi</b>

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.900
<b>It was such a happy surprise to see you today!
 I guess quiz bowl has kept us connected!
 We're in different high schools, 
but let's continue to hang out 
like we did in middle school! 
And good luck with your quiz bowl team! 
I'll do my best, too! Give my regards
 to the bookworm (lol) of Buzou High!</b>

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:30.580
Well, I switch trains here.

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:31.780
See you.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:33.150
Thanks for coming today!

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.600
<i>When I thought about quiz bowl,
what jumped into my head</i>

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:40.770
<i>was that it was a place where all kinds 
of pointless trivia came in handy.</i>

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:45.800
<i>When I was able to predict the rest 
of a question or buzz in first,</i>

00:20:46.030 --> 00:20:48.950
<i>I really did feel happy. But more so...</i>

00:20:48.950 --> 00:20:50.180
Koshiyama-kun!

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:55.730
Mikuriya-kun?

00:20:55.730 --> 00:20:58.150
I guess he lives in this direction, too.

00:21:00.500 --> 00:21:02.130
You think he's in a bad mood?

00:21:04.870 --> 00:21:08.000
<i>It must be because I put a 
damper on his big competition.</i>

00:21:08.540 --> 00:21:10.320
Did you decide about joining our circle?

00:21:11.090 --> 00:21:15.590
After today, I realized that 
I'm no good at this stuff at all.

00:21:15.590 --> 00:21:19.480
But I'll work hard. If I'm 
going to play, I want to win.

00:21:19.480 --> 00:21:22.130
I want to make our circle really strong!

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:26.340
And I think I need your help to do that.

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:32.060
I want to do quiz bowl 
with you, Koshiyama-kun.

00:21:35.000 --> 00:21:36.380
Well, I'm catching the bus now.

00:21:36.380 --> 00:21:38.080
See you at school. Bye-bye!

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:41.170
Bye... bye...

00:21:41.170 --> 00:21:43.210
Co-ed schools must be nice.

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:45.730
M-Mikuriya-kun?!

00:21:50.990 --> 00:21:53.620
The regular meet in June is up next.

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:54.330
Huh?

00:21:54.330 --> 00:21:55.590
Revenge!

00:21:56.580 --> 00:21:57.640
I'm telling you...

00:21:58.040 --> 00:21:59.540
to do quiz bowl!

00:22:02.590 --> 00:22:03.490
Do it!

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:13.330
<i>Seriously, why do all these people...</i>

00:22:13.630 --> 00:22:15.930
<i>All these things I never had before,</i>

00:22:15.930 --> 00:22:19.150
<i>things I never knew...</i>

00:22:19.150 --> 00:22:23.510
<i>things like fun, happiness, frustration...</i>

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:27.360
<i>and being able to share 
those things with others.</i>

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:34.040
The season of high school quiz 
bowl has come round again!

00:22:34.470 --> 00:22:39.040
High schoolers of Japan, start by pairing 
up to enter the All Japan Championship!

00:22:36.290 --> 00:22:39.040
<b>All Japan Championship</b>

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:40.540
Pairing up?

00:22:40.830 --> 00:22:44.470
Any two students from the same school can 
form a pair, even if they're in different grades.

00:22:44.470 --> 00:22:46.550
Of course, it can be a mixed pair, too.

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.050
Koshiyama-kun, let's enter together!

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:53.630
H-Huh?! No, I'm not ready for 
a real championship yet!

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:56.140
I'm not ready to be in a 
mixed pairing, either!

00:22:56.140 --> 00:22:57.560
There must be another step to take first...

00:22:57.560 --> 00:22:59.830
I don't care how you do it. 
Just do quiz bowl.

00:22:57.560 --> 00:23:00.350
<b>Do quiz bowl!!</b>
